 ## Overview
-Prefiltered Reconstruction is a volume rendering technique that improves the approximation order of non-interpolating filters, such as the cubic B-spline, by convolving input samples with a discrete prefilter before applying the continuous reconstruction kernel [S1]. It solves the problem of severe oversmoothing inherent in standard approximating filters, which can disconnect fine anatomical structures like blood vessels in CT scans [S1]. Unlike standard cubic B-spline interpolation, which acts as a low-pass filter that blurs high-frequency details, this method allows the cubic B-spline to act as an interpolator with $C^2$ continuity and 4th-order approximation accuracy (4-EF) [S1]. It sets itself apart from its parent [Fast tri-cubic interpolation](848-fast-tri-cubic-interpolation.md) by explicitly handling the coefficient generation step to correct for the non-interpolating nature of the basis function, and from its sibling [Cubic Interpolation (Mitchell-Netravali)](2073-cubic-interpolation-mitchell-netravali.md) by targeting the specific frequency response characteristics of B-splines rather than general cubic kernels.
